<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1780">1780</a>–<a href="/years/1867">1867</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ingres</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres was a French Neoclassical painter born in Montauban who studied in the studio of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacques-louis-david">Jacques-Louis David</a>. He won the Prix de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1802 and worked in Rome and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> from 1806 to 1824, supporting himself as a portrait painter while critics in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> found his style bizarre and archaic. The Vow of Louis XIII brought acclaim at the Salon of 1824, and he later directed the French Academy in Rome from 1835. He finished The Turkish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Bath</a> at the age of 83. His portraits are recognised as his chief legacy, and his distortions of form influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-matisse">Henri Matisse</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pablo-picasso">Pablo Picasso</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jean-auguste-dominique-ingres.jpg" alt="Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres" width="330" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ingres,_Self-portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
